    </div>
    <div class="decade-card">
      <div class="decade-label">2020s Average</div>
      <div class="decade-val">{d20:.2f}</div>
      <div class="decade-change {'trend-up' if pct_20>0 else 'trend-down'}">{pct_20:+.1f}% vs 2010s</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Countries Reporting per Year</h4>
      <div class="chart-container short"><canvas id="coverageChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Regional Median Rates Over Time</h4>
      <div class="chart-container short"><canvas id="regionTrendChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">The number of reporting countries has grown substantially from roughly 40–60 in the early 1990s to over 120 by the 2010s, before declining to {countries_2023} in 2023 — reflecting both genuine progress in data collection and reporting cycles. This expansion must be held in mind when interpreting trend data: part of the apparent change in global rates reflects changes in the composition of reporting countries rather than true underlying shifts.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">2</div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-title">Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</div>
      <div class="section-objective">Highest and lowest homicide rate countries, geographic patterns, and regional disparities.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">Regional disparities in homicide rates are among the most persistent patterns in the data. <strong>The Americas</strong> consistently record the highest median rates — typically 10–20× those of Europe and Asia — driven by concentrated violence in the Caribbean, Central America, and parts of South America. In contrast, <strong>Europe and Asia</strong> maintain the lowest medians, typically under 2.0 per 100,000.</p>

  <div class="insight-box warn">
    <strong>Americas outlier:</strong> The regional median for the Americas in 2023 was approximately 16.2 per 100,000 — over 18 times higher than Europe's median of 0.89. This single region accounts for a disproportionate share of global homicide burden and warrants targeted policy attention.
  </div>

  <div class="table-row">
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-bottom:10px">Top 10: Highest Rates (Most Recent Year per Country)</h4>
      <table class="mini-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>"""

for i, row in enumerate(top10.to_dict('records')):
    html += f"""
          <tr>
            <td>{row['country']}</td>
            <td>{row['year']}</td>
            <td><span class="rate-badge rate-high">{row['homicide_rate']:.1f}</span></td>
          </tr>"""

html += """
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-bottom:10px">Bottom 10: Lowest Rates (Most Recent Year per Country)</h4>
      <table class="mini-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>"""

for row in bottom10.to_dict('records'):
    html += f"""
          <tr>
            <td>{row['country']}</td>
            <td>{row['year']}</td>
            <td><span class="rate-badge rate-low">{row['homicide_rate']:.3f}</span></td>
          </tr>"""

html += f"""
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Top 10 Highest-Rate Countries</h4>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="top10Chart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Bottom 10 Lowest-Rate Countries</h4>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="bottom10Chart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text" style="margin-top:16px">Small island states — particularly in the Eastern Caribbean — feature prominently among the highest-rate countries, where even a single homicide can drive the per-capita rate to extreme levels given small populations. This statistical artifact underscores the importance of interpreting rates alongside absolute counts when setting policy priorities.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">3</div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-title">Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</div>
      <div class="section-objective">Male-to-female homicide rate ratio globally and its evolution over time.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">Homicide victimization is profoundly gendered. Across all years and geographies, male victims outnumber female victims by a ratio of approximately <strong>3–4:1</strong>. In the most recent year with disaggregated data ({mf_recent_year}), the global median male-to-female ratio stood at <strong>{global_mf_ratio:.2f}</strong>. This disparity is driven by multiple overlapping factors: higher male involvement in organized crime, interpersonal conflict, and gang violence.</p>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Gender disparity KPI:</strong> The male-to-female ratio of {global_mf_ratio:.2f} means that for every female homicide victim, approximately {global_mf_ratio:.1f} males are killed. This ratio has remained relatively stable over the observation period, suggesting structural rather than cyclical drivers.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container tall">
    <canvas id="genderTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">While the absolute rates for both sexes have generally trended downward in line with the global pattern, the ratio has remained persistent — suggesting that the underlying social and structural factors driving male victimization have not been fundamentally disrupted by crime reduction policies. Female homicide victimization, though lower in overall volume, may include a higher proportion of intimate-partner violence, which has distinct policy implications.</p>

  <div class="chart-container short">
    <canvas id="mfRatioChart"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: AGE GROUP ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">4</div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-title">Age Group Analysis</div>
      <div class="section-objective">Which age groups face the highest homicide risk, and whether youth or elderly populations are disproportionately affected.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">The UNODC dataset provides age-disaggregated rates for a subset of countries and years (2003–2023), with data available primarily in male and female breakdowns rather than combined totals. Analysis of male rates — which are more complete — reveals a clear age-risk gradient:</p>

  <div class="insight-box warn">
    <strong>Youth and young-adult peak:</strong> The 25–29 and 30–44 age groups consistently show the highest homicide rates across reporting countries, with rates approximately 5–12× higher than children (0–9) and markedly higher than those aged 60+. Young men in particular face acute risk.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Median Homicide Rate by Age Group (Male, All Years)</h4>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="ageChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.5px;margin-bottom:10px">Age Group Trend Over Time (Male Median Rate)</h4>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="ageTrendChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">Children (0–9) and early adolescents (10–14) face the lowest absolute homicide rates, though these figures can mask targeted violence against children in conflict zones with limited reporting. The 60+ cohort, while lower than working-age adults, still faces meaningful risk in high-rate countries. Policies targeting youth violence prevention — particularly for males aged 18–35 — are likely to yield the greatest reduction in overall homicide burden.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: NOTABLE TRENDS -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">5</div>
    <div>
      <div class="section-title">Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</div>
      <div class="section-objective">Countries with the most dramatic increases or decreases, and statistical outliers.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="body-text">Across countries with at least five years of data, the dataset reveals dramatic variation in trajectories. Some nations have achieved extraordinary reductions, while others have seen sustained deterioration over the observation period.</p>

  <div class="table-row">
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--green);margin-bottom:10px">Greatest Percentage Declines</h4>
      <table class="mini-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Country</th><th>Period</th><th>First Rate</th><th>Last Rate</th><th>Change</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>"""

for row in top_declines:
    html += f"""
          <tr>
            <td>{row['country']}</td>
            <td>{row['first_year']}–{row['last_year']}</td>
            <td>{row['first_rate']:.1f}</td>
            <td>{row['last_rate']:.2f}</td>
            <td class="trend-down">{row['pct_change']:+.0f}%</td>
          </tr>"""

html += """
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h4 style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:10px">Greatest Percentage Increases</h4>
