    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-bottom:12px">Data Limitations</h3>
  <div class="limits-grid">
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>Unbalanced Panel</h4>
      <p>Countries differ substantially in how many years they report. A simple average of all-country rates in any year reflects the composition of reporting countries that year, not a stable global benchmark. Trend interpretation requires awareness of which countries enter and exit the reporting pool.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>No Interpolation</h4>
      <p>Missing years are absent from the dataset rather than imputed. This means consecutive-year analysis can mask structural breaks. Countries with sparse data (fewer than 5 years) should be treated cautiously in trend comparisons.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>UK Coverage Note</h4>
      <p>United Kingdom data in this dataset refer to <strong>England and Wales only</strong>. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries. Analysts should not double-count UK totals or use GBR figures as representative of the full United Kingdom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>Small-Population Territories</h4>
      <p>Micro-territories (e.g., Turks and Caicos Islands, Montserrat, Saint Helena) can show extreme per-capita rates from a single event. These should be contextualised with absolute counts, not treated as comparable to sovereign state rates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>Age Group Coverage</h4>
      <p>Age-disaggregated data is available only for a subset of countries and years (primarily 2003 onward), and only in male/female disaggregation — not combined totals. The age analysis in Section 4 should be interpreted as a directional indicator, not a comprehensive global picture.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="limit-card">
      <h4>Reporting Incentives</h4>
      <p>UNODC data depend on national reporting, which may understate homicides in conflict zones, failed states, or regions with limited judicial infrastructure. High-burden countries are disproportionately likely to have missing data, potentially understating the true global mean.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;color:var(--primary);margin-top:24px;margin-bottom:12px">Policy Recommendations</h3>
  <div class="limits-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>1. Prioritise the Americas</h4>
      <p>Regional median rates 18× higher than Europe demand proportionate international cooperation, judicial reform, and violence prevention investment — particularly in the Caribbean and Andean subregions showing worsening trends.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>2. Target Young Male Victimization</h4>
      <p>The 18–35 male cohort drives the overwhelming majority of homicide victimization. Youth-focused programmes — employment, diversion, community policing, and social services — should be the primary channel for rate reduction.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>3. Strengthen Reporting Infrastructure</h4>
      <p>Only 42% of tracked countries reported in 2023. Expanding UNODC CTS participation — particularly in Sub-Saharan Africa and conflict-affected Asia — is essential for evidence-based global monitoring.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>4. Investigate Stagnation in the 2020s</h4>
      <p>The near-zero change (+0.2%) in mean homicide rates from the 2010s to 2020s warrants investigation. COVID-era social disruption, reduced policing, and economic stress may have reversed previous gains in vulnerable countries.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>5. Study El Salvador's Trajectory</h4>
      <p>The documented 94% decline in El Salvador's homicide rate — among the steepest in the dataset — offers a case study for policy learning, even accounting for the methodological and rights-related debates around enforcement approaches.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>6. Gender-Sensitive Approaches</h4>
      <p>The persistent 3–4:1 male-to-female homicide ratio suggests that gender-neutral policing strategies are insufficient. Male-targeted violence prevention must be integrated alongside domestic/intimate partner violence programmes to address both dimensions.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

